### 2.2 Deep Spiking Q-Network Architecture

A DSQN is implemented to select discrete stimulation parameters, modulating the current driven by the stimulator. A Q-network is a value-based reinforcement learning architecture that estimates the expected future cumulative reward (Q-value) associated with taking a specific action in a given state. We specifically selected this off-policy, discrete-action approach over continuous policy gradient methods (e.g., Proximal Policy Optimization or Actor-Critic) for three primary reasons: (i) clinical DBS neurostimulators inherently operate using discrete, safety-bounded parameter step adjustments rather than continuous analog values, making Q-learning structurally aligned with the hardware interface; (ii) its off-policy nature permits the use of an experience replay buffer, which substantially improves sample efficiency when training against computationally expensive biophysical simulations; and (iii) the deterministic argmax selection of maximum Q-values maps natively to a competitive winner-take-all (WTA) readout layer, an optimal decoding strategy for event-driven spiking neuromorphic cores. Simultaneous co-modulation of these variables governs both the side-effect profile [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.28600v1#bib.bib20) and battery longevity [42](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.28600v1#bib.bib21). The network is implemented in PyTorch [37](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.28600v1#bib.bib3) using the Rockpool neuromorphic library [30](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.28600v1#bib.bib22), which provides differentiable dynamics suitable for gradient-based optimization via surrogate gradients [10](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.28600v1#bib.bib4). The SNN is constructed using Leaky Integrate-and-Fire (LIF) neurons with explicit synaptic current dynamics. In the discrete-time forward Euler formulation used by Rockpool, the synaptic current IsI\_{s} and membrane potential VmV\_{m} evolve as a coupled system:

