where βt\beta\_{t} is the instantaneous β\beta-band power, EtE\_{t} is the instantaneous simulation RMS energy proxy (Eq. [7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.28600v1#S2.E7 "In 2.4 Action Space and Clinical Constraints ‣ 2 Methods ‣ Neuromorphic Energy-Aware Learning for Adaptive Deep Brain Stimulation")), EmaxE\_{\mathrm{max}} is a normalization constant, τreward\tau\_{\mathrm{reward}} is the base therapeutic reward, κ\kappa is the suppression penalty coefficient, and α\alpha is a hyperparameter balancing the target outcome against the energy-savings gradient. The energy term enters the reward as the normalized ratio Et/EmaxE\_{t}/E\_{\mathrm{max}} rather than an absolute current. The policy therefore optimizes the fraction of charge it delivers relative to its own operating envelope, and the learned savings are a proportion of the baseline rather than a fixed quantity. κ\kappa is matched to τreward\tau\_{\mathrm{reward}} to establish proportionate incentives for symptom suppression and threshold violation; both are set large enough to keep temporal difference errors in the L1L\_{1} regime of the Huber loss throughout early training, stabilizing gradient updates before the policy discovers the therapeutic regime. This formulation guarantees the agent only receives positive reinforcement when the β\beta-band pathological signature is sufficiently suppressed. We note that τβ\tau\_{\beta} is an empirically derived fixed absolute threshold used to shape the training reward. The complete deep spiking Q-learning procedure is summarized in Algorithm [1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.28600v1#alg1 "Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.5 Adaptive Reward Formulation ‣ 2 Methods ‣ Neuromorphic Energy-Aware Learning for Adaptive Deep Brain Stimulation"). Full implementation details, including the multi-head action structure, Q-value readout, and sparsity regularization, are provided in Algorithm [S1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.28600v1#alg1a "Algorithm S1 ‣ Appendix C EA-D