#### 3.4.2 Energy per Inference

Energy per inference is the product of mean inference power and execution latency. Accumulated over the lifetime of a continuously running controller, it is what drains the battery. The Xylo student model consumed 1.64×10−41.64\times 10^{-4} J per inference, compared to 4.61×10−34.61\times 10^{-3} J for the ANN and 1.19×10−21.19\times 10^{-2} J for the RNN on the Jetson CUDA, a 28.1×\times and 72.5×\times reduction respectively. The RNN’s higher energy reflects both its longer sequential inference and the Jetson’s sustained watts-level power draw. As with the power comparison, these ratios set the Xylo core against the Jetson’s full system.

